    "knowledge_point": "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola Whose Vertex Is at the Origin",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030835/assets/85894c676349da1794c2b4e3ab3add867fbe9e91037bee060e3592039c3850cf.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?",
    "worked_solution": "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-4,4).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ are\n\n$$\nx=p{t}^{2},\\,y=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-4,4),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=4$ into the standard parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx=(-1){t}^{2},\\,y=2(-1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx=-{t}^{2},\\,y=-2t.\n$$",
